{
  "stage": "write",
  "item": "ch02.s14",
  "inputs_hash": "d6d544dc7c666b8b1a9a04a133566e5029a3d0095e03a1aeba0c5962df26c366",
  "output_hash": "59881853fb129dbde34cc2759e204f821f6d67184d6a1c667a7f0ff87500afd9",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch02.s14",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch02.s14.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p01.01",
            "text": "How do token IDs become the actual vectors a large language model receives, and why is position part of that transformation?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p01.02",
            "text": "After tokenization and token IDs, the next preprocessing steps are token embeddings and positional embeddings.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@2920",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2923",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2925"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p01.03",
            "text": "Token embeddings convert token IDs into vectors, and these token IDs are encoded into a higher-dimensional vector space.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@163",
              "mk-6cFebjis@165",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2944",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2947",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2950",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2953"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p01.04",
            "text": "Positional embeddings encode information about the position.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@168",
              "mk-6cFebjis@170",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5574",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5578",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5580"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p01.05",
            "text": "They are needed in addition to token embeddings because token embeddings alone do not include positional information, and that additional positional information is helpful for large language models.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5033",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5038",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5041",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5043",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5045",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5570",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5572",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5574"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p01.06",
            "text": "To create input embeddings, positional embeddings are added to token embeddings, and those input embeddings are the final input to the large language model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@2954",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2956",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2958",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2961",
              "mk-6cFebjis@125",
              "mk-6cFebjis@128",
              "mk-6cFebjis@130",
              "mk-6cFebjis@136",
              "mk-6cFebjis@138"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p01.07",
            "text": "In short, input embeddings are the sum of token embeddings and positional embeddings.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@125",
              "mk-6cFebjis@128",
              "mk-6cFebjis@130",
              "mk-6cFebjis@136",
              "mk-6cFebjis@138",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5480",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5482"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s14.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.01",
            "text": "Before constructing those vectors in code, it helps to distinguish the main kinds of positional information.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.02",
            "text": "The terms positional embedding and positional encoding are used interchangeably here.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@4938",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4940",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4942",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5366",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5368",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5370"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.03",
            "text": "There are two types of positional encoding: absolute positional encoding and relative positional encoding.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5050",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5052",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5054",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5057"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.04",
            "text": "In absolute positional encoding, each position in the input sequence has a unique positional embedding vector that is added to the token embedding.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5058",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5060",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5062",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5065",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5067"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.05",
            "text": "For example, with the tokens \"cat\", \"sat\", \"on\", and \"the\", each token can start from a similar token embedding, but the first, second, third, and fourth positions receive different positional embedding vectors.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5072",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5076",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5079",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5081",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5085",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5089",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5091",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5093",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5095",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5101",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5103",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5105",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5107",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5109",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5111",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5114",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5116",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5117"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.06",
            "text": "Adding those positional vectors to the token embeddings produces the final input embeddings.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5134",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5137",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5139",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5141",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5155",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5158",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5160"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.07",
            "text": "Because position is encoded, the final input embeddings can differ even when the token embeddings are similar.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5162",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5164",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5165",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5167"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.08",
            "text": "For the same reason, the input embeddings for \"cat sat on the mat\" differ from those for \"the mat the cat sat on\" when position is taken into account.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5170",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5172",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5175",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5177"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p02.09",
            "text": "The positional embedding vectors must have the same dimension as the token embeddings because the two vectors are added together.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5177",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5180",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5182",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5183",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5185"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s14.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p03.01",
            "text": "Relative positional encoding emphasizes a different notion of position.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p03.02",
            "text": "Relative positional encoding focuses on the relative positions or distances between tokens rather than their exact positions.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5188",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5190",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5193",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5194",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5197",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5200",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5203",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5205",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5207"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p03.03",
            "text": "Absolute positional encoding is well suited when the fixed order of tokens is crucial, such as sequence generation.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5215",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5217",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5219",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5222"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p03.04",
            "text": "GPT uses absolute positional encoding, and absolute positional encoding is more commonly used than relative positional encoding.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5222",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5225",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5226"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p03.05",
            "text": "Relative positional encoding is suitable for tasks like language modeling or long sequences where the same phrase can appear in different parts of the sequence.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5231",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5233",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5235",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5238",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5240",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5241",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5244"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s14.p04",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.01",
            "text": "With those definitions in place, the practical construction is straightforward.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.02",
            "text": "For a batch size of eight, a context length of four, and an embedding dimension of 256, the token embedding layer produces token embeddings with shape 8 by 4 by 256.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@4903",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4905",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4908",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4911",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4913",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4918",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4920",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4922",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4925"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.03",
            "text": "The token embeddings for one batch are then combined with positional embeddings, and that sum becomes the input to the large language model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@4926",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4928",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4930",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4933",
              "mk-6cFebjis@4936"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.04",
            "text": "The next step is to create a separate embedding layer for positional embeddings.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5250",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5253",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5256",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5262",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5264",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5266"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.05",
            "text": "When creating the positional embedding matrix, the relevant number of positions is determined by the context length.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5270",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5274",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5277",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5278",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5294",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5296",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5299"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.06",
            "text": "Here the positional embedding dimension is 256, matching the token embedding dimension, because the positional and token vectors must be added.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5280",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5282",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5285",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5287",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5289",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5291"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.07",
            "text": "With a context length of four, only four positional vectors are needed, one for each possible token position.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5294",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5296",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5299",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5301",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5303",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5306",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5307",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5309",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5312"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.08",
            "text": "Therefore, the positional embedding matrix has shape 4 by 256.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5312",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5314",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5316",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5319",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5321",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5325",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5328"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p04.09",
            "text": "In code, the positional embedding layer is created with four rows and 256 columns, and the four positional vectors are obtained from it.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5358",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5360",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5363",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5374",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5376",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5378",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5381",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5383",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5386",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5387",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5390",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5392",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5395",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5397"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s14.p05",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p05.01",
            "text": "The final step is the addition itself.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p05.02",
            "text": "The same four positional vectors are added to each input sequence in the batch.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5330",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5333",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5336",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5338",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5343",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5345",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5348",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5452",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5455",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5464",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5466"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p05.03",
            "text": "After creating the positional embeddings, they are added to the token embeddings.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5400",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5401",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5404",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5406",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5407"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p05.04",
            "text": "When adding token embeddings of shape 8 by 4 by 256 and positional embeddings of shape 4 by 256 in Python, broadcasting duplicates the positional embeddings across the batch dimension to make the addition possible.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5409",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5413",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5416",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5418",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5420",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5421",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5424",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5426",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5429"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p05.05",
            "text": "The input embedding matrix is constructed by adding the positional embedding vectors to each row of the token embedding matrix to produce the corresponding rows of the input embeddings.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5431",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5433",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5436",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5439",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5441",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5443",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5446",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5450",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5452",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5455",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5457",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5460",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5462",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5464",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5466",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5468",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5470",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5472",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5474"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p05.06",
            "text": "The positional embedding matrix is initialized randomly, similar to the token embedding matrix.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5486",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5488",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5491",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5493",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5503",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5506",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5508",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5510",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5512"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s14.p05.07",
            "text": "Like the values in the token embedding layer, the values in the positional embedding layer must be optimized during training.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@5512",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5515",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5516",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5518",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5527",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5529",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5530",
              "mk-6cFebjis@5532"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
